\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 # HYCO CRM \f1\emdash  Master Project Context & Development Prompt\par
\par
## 1. Project Overview\par
\par
I am building a custom Lead Management CRM for HYCO. The CRM should provide a workflow similar to TeleCRM, especially its lead management interface, lead assignment, WhatsApp communication, call activity tracking, follow-ups, notes, and activity history.\par
\par
The goal is to manage leads from multiple sources in one centralized system and allow the team to communicate with leads, track every activity, and manage the complete lead lifecycle.\par
\par
**Important:** This is primarily a lead-management CRM, not an e-commerce application.\par
\par
Do not prioritize website orders, inventory, payment gateways, or order processing unless I explicitly request them later.\par
\par
## 2. Core Business Strategy\par
\par
The central concept is:\par
\par
**Lead acquisition \f2\u8594?\f0  Lead management \f2\u8594?\f0  Assignment \f2\u8594?\f0  Communication \f2\u8594?\f0  Follow-up \f2\u8594?\f0  Status progression \f2\u8594?\f0  Conversion \f2\u8594?\f0  Reporting**\par
\par
Leads may come from:\par
- Manual single-lead entry.\par
- Excel/CSV file imports.\par
- WhatsApp conversations.\par
- Facebook lead data imported through Excel files.\par
\par
Do not implement direct Facebook API integration unless explicitly requested.\par
\par
All lead sources should ultimately create or update records in the same lead-management system.\par
\par
## 3. Main CRM Features\par
\par
### A. Lead Management\par
\par
The CRM must support:\par
- Add a single lead.\par
- Import multiple leads through Excel/CSV.\par
- View all leads.\par
- Search leads by name, phone, email, and other supported fields.\par
- Filter and sort leads.\par
- View individual lead details.\par
- Edit lead information.\par
- Assign and reassign leads.\par
- Update lead status.\par
- Add notes.\par
- Schedule follow-ups.\par
- View complete lead activity history.\par
- Prevent or flag duplicate leads.\par
- Support bulk actions where appropriate.\par
\par
Lead fields may include:\par
- Name.\par
- Phone number.\par
- Alternate phone number.\par
- Email.\par
- State.\par
- City.\par
- Address.\par
- Product or service of interest.\par
- Quantity.\par
- Lead source.\par
- Lead status.\par
- Assigned employee, if assignment is enabled.\par
- Notes.\par
- Creation date.\par
- Last activity date.\par
- Next follow-up date.\par
\par
Keep fields configurable where practical. Do not make every field mandatory without a business requirement.\par
\par
### B. Lead Sources\par
\par
Supported lead sources:\par
1. Manual entry.\par
2. Excel/CSV import.\par
3. WhatsApp.\par
4. Facebook data imported through Excel.\par
\par
Store the source of each lead so that reports can distinguish between channels.\par
\par
For imported leads, preserve import-batch information when useful, such as filename, import date, and row number.\par
\par
### C. Excel/CSV Import Strategy\par
\par
Implement a reliable import workflow:\par
\par
1. User selects and uploads a file.\par
2. Validate file type and size.\par
3. Parse and preview the data.\par
4. Map spreadsheet columns to CRM fields.\par
5. Validate phone numbers and other supported fields.\par
6. Detect existing or duplicate leads.\par
7. Show valid rows and errors before import.\par
8. Allow the user to choose an appropriate duplicate-handling strategy.\par
9. Import valid records.\par
10. Display an import summary, including created records, skipped duplicates, and failed rows.\par
\par
Avoid silently discarding invalid data. Never execute spreadsheet formulas or treat spreadsheet contents as trusted instructions.\par
\par
### D. WhatsApp Integration\par
\par
The business requirement is to use **WhatsApp Web-based integration, not the WhatsApp Cloud API**.\par
\par
The intended workflow is:\par
\par
1. An authorized CRM user connects a WhatsApp account by scanning a QR code.\par
2. The connection status is visible in the CRM.\par
3. Incoming messages are received.\par
4. The CRM identifies an existing lead using the phone number.\par
5. If no matching lead exists, the system can create a new lead according to the configured workflow.\par
6. The conversation and messages are associated with the lead.\par
7. Users can view conversation history and relevant lead details.\par
8. WhatsApp activity is recorded in the lead's activity timeline.\par
9. Users can initiate permitted WhatsApp actions from the lead detail screen.\par
\par
Use a suitable, actively maintained integration approach after verifying compatibility and security.\par
\par
Important technical considerations:\par
- WhatsApp Web automation libraries may be unofficial and can break when WhatsApp changes its implementation.\par
- Account restrictions or blocking are possible.\par
- Sessions must be stored securely.\par
- QR authentication must not expose credentials or session files.\par
- Handle reconnects, disconnections, duplicate messages, and errors.\par
- Do not claim that messages were delivered or read unless that status is actually available.\par
- Never invent delivery statuses.\par
- Do not integrate the Cloud API unless I explicitly change this requirement.\par
\par
### E. Call Logs\par
\par
The CRM should support call activity tracking when a real source of call information is available.\par
\par
Possible call log fields:\par
- Lead.\par
- Phone number.\par
- Call direction.\par
- Call status.\par
- Start time.\par
- End time.\par
- Duration.\par
- Employee/user.\par
- Notes or disposition.\par
- Recording reference, if available and legally permitted.\par
\par
Distinguish between:\par
- Displaying call logs.\par
- Initiating actual phone calls.\par
- Receiving call events from a telephony provider.\par
- Recording or storing call recordings.\par
\par
These are separate capabilities. Do not claim that actual calling or recording works without a compatible provider or device integration.\par
\par
Do not add a telephony provider or paid service without discussing the requirement first.\par
\par
### F. Follow-ups and Tasks\par
\par
Users should be able to:\par
- Schedule a follow-up against a lead.\par
- Select date and time.\par
- Choose a follow-up type, such as call, WhatsApp, email, or meeting.\par
- Add notes.\par
- Mark a follow-up completed or cancelled.\par
- View upcoming, overdue, and completed follow-ups.\par
- View follow-up history from the lead detail page.\par
\par
If notifications are implemented, trigger them from actual follow-up events and respect the configured notification preferences.\par
\par
### G. Lead Activity History\par
\par
Every important lead-related action should be recorded in a chronological timeline.\par
\par
Examples:\par
- Lead created.\par
- Lead imported from Excel.\par
- Lead assigned or reassigned.\par
- Lead status changed.\par
- Note added.\par
- Follow-up scheduled, completed, or cancelled.\par
- WhatsApp message received or sent.\par
- Call initiated or logged.\par
- Lead information updated.\par
\par
Activity records should include the action type, timestamp, relevant lead, and actor when known.\par
\par
System-generated activities should be distinguishable from actions performed by users.\par
\par
Avoid storing unnecessary sensitive message content in general-purpose audit logs.\par
\par
### H. Lead Status Management\par
\par
Lead statuses should represent the business's actual sales workflow.\par
\par
Possible starting statuses:\par
- New.\par
- Contacted.\par
- Qualified.\par
- Proposal.\par
- Negotiation.\par
- Won.\par
- Lost.\par
\par
These are proposed defaults, not mandatory final business rules. Keep statuses configurable or confirm the exact list before hardcoding it.\par
\par
Status transitions must be validated. If a lead is marked Won or Lost, preserve the history of the status change.\par
\par
Do not confuse a lead's sales status with a WhatsApp conversation status or a follow-up status.\par
\par
### I. Lead Assignment\par
\par
If assignment is enabled, support:\par
- Assigning leads to a user.\par
- Reassigning leads.\par
- Viewing leads assigned to a user.\par
- Filtering by assignee.\par
- Optional bulk assignment.\par
- Optional balanced distribution of imported leads.\par
\par
If automatic distribution is introduced, define its rules explicitly. Do not silently assign leads based on arbitrary database ordering.\par
\par
### J. Lead Detail Page\par
\par
The lead detail page should bring important information together:\par
- Contact details.\par
- Source.\par
- Status.\par
- Assignment.\par
- Product and quantity.\par
- Notes.\par
- Follow-ups.\par
- WhatsApp conversation.\par
- Call history.\par
- Activity timeline.\par
\par
The interface should be inspired by the practical workflow visible in TeleCRM, but the implementation must use our own code and design.\par
\par
## 4. Technical Architecture\par
\par
The existing backend was started with:\par
- JavaScript.\par
- Node.js.\par
- Express.js.\par
- MongoDB.\par
- Mongoose.\par
- JWT authentication.\par
- bcryptjs for password hashing.\par
- ESM modules using `"type": "module"`.\par
\par
The frontend is a separate application. Inspect the actual repository before assuming the frontend framework, file structure, or API conventions.\par
\par
Backend conventions:\par
- Use JavaScript, not TypeScript, unless I explicitly request otherwise.\par
- Keep the Express application/server setup in `src/server.js` if that is the current project structure.\par
- Use Mongoose models for persistent data.\par
- Keep routes, controllers, models, and middleware organized.\par
- Use environment variables for secrets and deployment-specific configuration.\par
- Validate incoming request data.\par
- Handle expected errors with appropriate HTTP status codes.\par
- Protect endpoints that require authentication.\par
- Avoid exposing passwords, tokens, or private session data in responses or logs.\par
\par
Do not create extra architecture layers merely for the sake of abstraction. Add services, helpers, or other modules only when they solve a real problem.\par
\par
## 5. Authentication, Roles, and Permissions\par
\par
The earlier backend included three roles:\par
- super_admin\par
- manager\par
- sales_employee\par
\par
However, the latest UI-cleanup requirement is to remove the previous role system if the current business workflow does not need it.\par
\par
**Do not assume role-based permissions are still required. Inspect the latest code and confirm the intended access model before removing roles.**\par
\par
Removing roles requires reviewing:\par
- User schema.\par
- JWT/authentication logic.\par
- Authorization middleware.\par
- Route-level role checks.\par
- Frontend role-dependent rendering.\par
- Lead assignment and access restrictions.\par
\par
Do not simply delete a role field while leaving incompatible middleware or route checks.\par
\par
If authentication is still required, preserve secure login and password hashing. Never hardcode production credentials.\par
\par
## 6. Database and Mock Data\par
\par
The final application must use persistent database records rather than frontend-only dummy data.\par
\par
Before connecting or changing the database:\par
1. Inspect the current project.\par
2. Identify mock data and hardcoded arrays.\par
3. Identify the API calls already implemented.\par
4. Identify the existing database models and routes.\par
5. Replace mock data with real API responses.\par
6. Implement appropriate loading, empty, and error states.\par
7. Verify create, read, update, and delete operations where applicable.\par
8. Avoid deleting real database records during cleanup.\par
\par
The database connection will be configured by the project owner. Never expose database credentials or commit `.env` files.\par
\par
Do not seed demo records in production.\par
\par
## 7. UI and Login Cleanup\par
\par
The CRM should provide a professional lead-management interface with:\par
- Lead list and search.\par
- Filters.\par
- Lead details.\par
- Notes and activity history.\par
- Follow-ups.\par
- WhatsApp controls.\par
- Excel import.\par
- Clear loading, empty, success, and error states.\par
\par
The login screen must not display demo usernames, demo passwords, or hardcoded credentials.\par
\par
Password fields should be masked by default. Do not display a user's actual password in the interface.\par
\par
Preserve working features and existing styling unless a change is necessary.\par
\par
## 8. Security and Reliability\par
\par
Follow these principles:\par
- Validate and sanitize user input.\par
- Use authentication and authorization appropriate to the final access model.\par
- Prevent unauthorized access to other users' leads where required.\par
- Validate file uploads and limit file size.\par
- Prevent duplicate imports and duplicate WhatsApp message processing.\par
- Protect WhatsApp session credentials.\par
- Do not trust client-side validation alone.\par
- Avoid logging secrets or unnecessary personal information.\par
- Use transactions or other consistency protections where multi-step operations could leave partial records.\par
- Add rate limiting and other production protections where appropriate.\par
- Never use temporary or demo endpoints as substitutes for real application functionality.\par
\par
## 9. Development Rules\par
\par
These rules are important:\par
\par
1. Inspect the existing repository before making changes.\par
2. Do not rewrite the entire project without a clear reason.\par
3. Do not invent files, functions, routes, or schemas that have not been checked.\par
4. Reuse existing working functionality wherever possible.\par
5. Do not add unnecessary demo APIs, health-check APIs, or temporary test features.\par
6. Do not implement unrelated order management, inventory, payments, or e-commerce features.\par
7. Do not integrate Facebook APIs or WhatsApp Cloud API unless explicitly requested.\par
8. Provide exact file paths and complete code for necessary changes.\par
9. Explain which files need modification and why.\par
10. Test the actual workflow after changes.\par
11. Identify breaking changes before applying them.\par
12. Never claim a feature is complete without verifying it.\par
\par
## 10. Current Project Status\par
\par
The earlier backend implementation included:\par
- Authentication and JWT.\par
- User management.\par
- Lead management.\par
- Lead-to-customer conversion.\par
- Customer records.\par
- Follow-ups.\par
- A provider-neutral WhatsApp webhook and conversation/message storage.\par
\par
Some of these modules may need modification to match the latest lead-centric scope.\par
\par
Do not assume every previous implementation is production-ready or still required. Inspect the current code before deciding what to retain, modify, or remove.\par
\par
The latest requested cleanup includes:\par
- Remove mock data.\par
- Connect the interface to persistent database/API data.\par
- Remove the old role system if it is no longer needed.\par
- Remove visible demo credentials from the login screen.\par
\par
The next step is to inspect the current repository and produce a change plan before modifying code.\par
\par
## 11. Priorities\par
\par
Recommended implementation order:\par
\par
1. Inspect the current codebase.\par
2. Confirm the final authentication/access model.\par
3. Remove mock data and connect existing screens to real APIs.\par
4. Finalize lead fields and statuses.\par
5. Finalize manual lead creation.\par
6. Finalize Excel/CSV import with preview, mapping, and duplicate handling.\par
7. Verify lead assignment and filtering.\par
8. Implement or refine WhatsApp Web integration.\par
9. Complete follow-ups and tasks.\par
10. Build the lead activity timeline.\par
11. Integrate call logs if a real call-data source is available.\par
12. Finalize reporting and dashboard data.\par
13. Test security, reliability, and deployment.\par
\par
## 12. How to Work With Me\par
\par
I prefer practical, direct development help.\par
\par
When modifying the project:\par
- Explain the concept briefly.\par
- Inspect the actual files.\par
- Give exact file paths.\par
- Provide complete replacement code when needed.\par
- Keep the implementation simple and production-oriented.\par
- Avoid unnecessary theory and repeated explanations.\par
- Ask questions only when a missing business decision materially affects the implementation.\par
- If a requirement is ambiguous, state your assumption and wait before making a destructive change.\par
\par
**First task:** Inspect the current repository and summarize the existing frontend, backend, database models, routes, mock data, authentication, and WhatsApp implementation. Then propose a minimal, safe cleanup plan. Do not start a full rewrite before that review.\lang9\par
}
